---

# Follow-up — the rename left a STALE inbox_url; inbound follow silently lost

The rename fixed `username`, `uri` and `url` but **not `inbox_url`**. A follow
directed at the new handle was delivered by mastodon.social to the *old* inbox
path, which 404s, so it vanished with no error on either side. Operator-reported
symptom: the mastodon.social profile page shows "follow requested" forever.

## Symptom

- Operator sees "Requested" on the mastodon.social admin profile page.
- Our side: `FollowRequest.count == 0`, no `Follow` row, **no inbox POST at all**
  (last delivery 23:11:37, the bot follow).
- The remote *sent* something; it never reached us.

## Cause

    $ rails runner   (admin account, id 117363090403638110)
    username=admin
    uri=https://mastodon.300x3.com/users/admin
    url=https://mastodon.300x3.com/users/admin
    inbox_url=https://mastodon.300x3.com/users/aoadmin/inbox   <-- STALE
    outbox_url=""                                                <-- EMPTY
    shared_inbox_url="https://mastodon.300x3.com/inbox"

`shared_inbox_url` was populated, so delivery went to the **per-account** stale
`inbox_url` rather than the shared `/inbox` that works.

    $ curl -X POST .../users/aoadmin/inbox   -> 404   (dead path)
    $ curl -X POST .../users/admin/inbox     -> 401   (alive; wants a signature)

A 404 on the remote delivery target is silent from the sender's perspective.

## Fix

    Account.update!(inbox_url: "https://mastodon.300x3.com/users/admin/inbox",
                    outbox_url: "https://mastodon.300x3.com/users/admin/outbox")

Verified live: the actor document advertises the matching inbox/outbox, and
`POST .../users/admin/inbox` returns 401 (alive) rather than 404.

## Audit: all local accounts are now self-consistent

    mastodon.internal  uri=/users/mastodon.internal  inbox matches
    admin              uri=/users/admin              inbox matches
    bot                uri=/users/bot                inbox matches

`bot` and `mastodon.internal` have empty `outbox_url`; that is normal in
Mastodon (outbox is optional) and their inboxes agree, so not a fault.

## Traps — the general lesson

- **A username/uri rename in Mastodon does NOT rewrite `inbox_url` or
  `outbox_url`.** Same class as the `uri` trap above, one level deeper: I fixed
  `uri`/`url` and stopped, without checking the sibling columns.
- **Always cross-check `inbox_url` against `uri` after any account rename.** A
  remote that cannot reach the inbox loses the activity *silently* — no NACK, no
  local log line, because the POST never arrives.
- **`shared_inbox_url` being set does not save you** — Mastodon prefers the
  per-account inbox when one is present.
- A 404 on an inbound POST is invisible to the sender. Only comparing "remote
  says sent" against "our inbox log is empty" reveals it.
- `Setting.reserved_usernames` is what made the rename possible; it is a
  setting, not a hardcoded Mastodon restriction (README previously claimed
  otherwise and has been corrected).

## Not done

- The follow still has to be re-sent by the operator; the delivery target is
  correct as of now.
- The stale `admin` cache on mastodon.social (id 117327389970897359) now points
  at the correct URI, so it should re-resolve; purging it would require an
  authenticated delete on a third-party server (operator approval).

  actor document); it does not make the old *WebFinger handle* resolve. Remote
  servers follow the alias when re-resolving, not by probing the old handle.
- `users.username` and `accounts.acct` do not exist in 4.3 (use
  `Account.find_by(username:, domain:)`); `Status#local_only` does not exist
  either (the column is `local`).
- A 404 from WebFinger means "not resolvable **now**", not "never existed" - a
  remote server may still have cached the actor. Check the DB and the remote cache
  before concluding an account never existed. (I got this wrong earlier.)

